var documenterSearchIndex = {"docs":
[{"category":"section","location":"manual/first-pack/#Your-first-pack","page":"Your first pack","text":"In this chapter you'll build a pack of readings, write it as JSON and read it back.\n\nA room thermostat reports three things: the room temperature, its setpoint and how hard the heating is working. The first record sets a base name, a base time and a base unit, and the two records after it use them too:\n\n# The first record sets a base name, a base time and a base unit. The records\n# after it use them too.\npack = Pack([\n    Record(bn = \"urn:dev:mac:0024befffe804ff1:\", bt = 1_700_000_000, bu = \"Cel\",\n           n = \"room\", v = 21.5),\n    Record(n = \"setpoint\", v = 21.0),\n    Record(n = \"heating\", u = \"/\", v = 0.4),\n])\n\njson = SenMLFormat.Json.encode(pack)\nprintln(String(copy(json)))\n\nback = SenMLFormat.Json.decode(json)\n@assert back == pack\nprintln(\"read back \", length(back), \" records\")\n[{\"bn\":\"urn:dev:mac:0024befffe804ff1:\",\"bt\":1700000000,\"bu\":\"Cel\",\"n\":\"room\",\"v\":21.5},{\"n\":\"setpoint\",\"v\":21},{\"n\":\"heating\",\"u\":\"/\",\"v\":0.4}]\nread back 3 records\n\nA few things to notice:\n\nRecord takes keywords named after the SenML labels (bn, n, u, v, ...). Every field you leave out is nothing.\n\"heating\" sets its own unit (/, a ratio from 0 to 1), which beats the base unit for that record only.\nThe JSON has no spaces, and numbers use the shortest text that reads back as the same value: 21 rather than 21.0.\ndecode gives back a Pack equal to the one you wrote.","title":"Your first pack"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/first-pack/#Picking-the-device-name","page":"Your first pack","text":"SenML names should be unique around the world, so the base name usually holds a device ID, such as a MAC address in a URN (urn:dev:mac:..., RFC 8428 section 4.5.1). The base name and the name are joined to make the full name, here urn:dev:mac:0024befffe804ff1:room. The full name may only use letters, digits and : . / _ -, and must start with a letter or digit.\n\n","title":"Picking the device name"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/untrusted-input/#Untrusted-input","page":"Untrusted input","text":"This chapter covers reading SenML that comes from a network or a device you don't control.\n\nEvery decoder is built for hostile input. It throws a SenMLError, and nothing else, rather than crashing, looping forever or asking for huge amounts of memory because of a few bad bytes:\n\nCut-off and changed input. The tests feed every test case, cut at every length and changed at random, to every decoder and both stream decoders (test/robustness.jl).\nDeep nesting. An extension value nested very deep in JSON or CBOR is refused or skipped without running out of stack.\nHuge lengths. A CBOR array that claims 2^64 records, or a byte string that claims 4 GiB, fails cleanly when the input runs out.\nXML entities. A DTD is refused, so entity expansion (\"billion laughs\") can't happen. Only UTF-8 is accepted.\nEXI. The strict SenML schema allows only two levels, and EXIFormat's decoder limits are on.","title":"Untrusted input"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/untrusted-input/#Checking-before-use","page":"Untrusted input","text":"decode validates the pack, so what you get back follows RFC 8428's rules: one value per record, valid names, finite numbers, a consistent version. Use decode_unchecked only for input you already trust.\n\nA valid pack can still be wrong for your application. Check the things only you know: the names you expect, the range of each value, and whether the times make sense.","title":"Checking before use"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/untrusted-input/#Limits-on-size","page":"Untrusted input","text":"SenMLFormat reads whatever input you give it. Put a limit on the message size before you call decode (CoAP and HTTP servers usually have one), or use a StreamDecoder and stop when you have read enough records.\n\n","title":"Limits on size"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/resolve/#Base-fields-and-resolving","page":"Base fields and resolving","text":"In this chapter you'll see how base fields and relative times work, and how to turn records into full names and absolute times.\n\nA home water meter reports its total use (a sum, in litres) every 10 minutes. The first record sets the base name, base time, base unit and a base sum. The times are relative to the base time:\n\n# The meter sends its total (a sum, in litres) every 10 minutes. The times are\n# relative to the base time: -1200 s, -600 s and 0 s.\npack = Pack([\n    Record(bn = \"urn:dev:mac:0024befffe804ff2:water:\", bt = 1_700_000_000, bu = \"l\",\n           bs = 48_000, n = \"total\", t = -1200, s = 12.5),\n    Record(n = \"total\", t = -600, s = 19.0),\n    Record(n = \"total\", s = 31.5),\n])\n\n# Resolving gives every record its full name, unit and time, and adds the base\n# sum. \"now\" is only needed for times below 2^28, which this pack does not use.\nfor r in resolve(pack)\n    println(r.name, \" t=\", round(Int, r.time), \" u=\", repr(r.unit), \" sum=\", r.sum)\nend\nurn:dev:mac:0024befffe804ff2:water:total t=1699998800 u=\"l\" sum=48012.5\nurn:dev:mac:0024befffe804ff2:water:total t=1699999400 u=\"l\" sum=48019.0\nurn:dev:mac:0024befffe804ff2:water:total t=1700000000 u=\"l\" sum=48031.5\n\nResolving follows RFC 8428 section 4.6:\n\nName: base name + name.\nUnit: the record's unit, else the base unit.\nTime: base time + time. A result below 2^28 is relative to \"now\", so it needs the now you pass in (seconds since 1970). Resolving a relative time without now throws a SenMLError of kind :RelativeTime.\nValue: base value + value, for numbers only.\nSum: base sum + sum.\nThe resolved records come out in time order.\n\nA record can hold base fields and nothing else. It sets context for the records after it and resolves to nothing.","title":"Base fields and resolving"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/resolve/#Record-by-record","page":"Base fields and resolving","text":"resolve works on a whole pack. For a stream, use a Resolver: give it each record as it arrives with resolve!, and change \"now\" with set_now! when you need to.","title":"Record by record"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/resolve/#Picking-records-out","page":"Base fields and resolving","text":"A RecordSelector reads the rec= fragment identifiers of section 9, such as rec=3-5, and selected tells you which positions they pick. A record's meaning depends on the base fields before it, so resolve the whole pack first, then pick.\n\n","title":"Picking records out"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/getting-started/#Getting-started","page":"Getting started","text":"In this chapter you'll add SenMLFormat to a project and run the examples.","title":"Getting started"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/getting-started/#Add-SenMLFormat-to-your-project","page":"Getting started","text":"SenMLFormat needs Julia 1.10 or newer.\n\nusing Pkg\nPkg.add(\"SenMLFormat\")\n\nThen:\n\nusing SenMLFormat\n\nThis brings the data model into scope: Record, Pack, validate, resolve, the media types and the rest. Each format is a module, SenMLFormat.Json, SenMLFormat.Cbor, SenMLFormat.Xml and SenMLFormat.Exi, named so they don't clash with the JSON.jl and XML.jl packages. SenMLFormat.decode and SenMLFormat.encode, which pick the format by media type, are not exported because the names are so common.","title":"Add SenMLFormat to your project"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/getting-started/#Run-the-examples","page":"Getting started","text":"The examples live in examples/basics of the repository, with their own Project.toml:\n\ngit clone https://github.com/nhenehan/SenMLFormat.jl && cd SenMLFormat.jl\njulia --project=examples/basics -e 'using Pkg; Pkg.develop(path=\".\")'   # once\njulia --project=examples/basics examples/basics/first_pack.jl\njulia --project=examples/basics examples/basics/run-all.jl              # every example","title":"Run the examples"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/getting-started/#The-calls-you'll-use-most","page":"Getting started","text":"Task Call\nBuild a record Record(n = \"room\", u = \"Cel\", v = 21.5)\nBuild a pack Pack([r1, r2])\nWrite one format SenMLFormat.Json.encode(pack) (also Cbor, Xml, Exi)\nRead one format SenMLFormat.Json.decode(bytes)\nPick the format at run time SenMLFormat.encode(media, pack), SenMLFormat.decode(media, bytes)\nCheck a pack validate(pack)\nFull names and times resolve(pack; now)\n\ndecode checks the pack as it reads, and encode checks it before writing. The _unchecked versions skip that, for packs you know are valid.\n\n","title":"The calls you'll use most"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/glossary/#Words-you'll-see","page":"Words you'll see","text":"This page explains the SenML words the manual uses, so you can come back to it when one is new.","title":"Words you'll see"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/glossary/#Record","page":"Words you'll see","text":"One entry in a SenML list. It can hold a name (n), a unit (u), a value, a sum (s), a time (t) and an update time (ut). The value is a number (v), a string (vs), a boolean (vb) or bytes (vd). In SenMLFormat it is a Record.","title":"Record"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/glossary/#Pack","page":"Words you'll see","text":"The whole list of records, sent as one message. In SenMLFormat it is a Pack, an AbstractVector{Record}.","title":"Pack"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/glossary/#Base-field","page":"Words you'll see","text":"A field whose name starts with b: base name (bn), base time (bt), base unit (bu), base value (bv), base sum (bs) and base version (bver). It applies to its own record and to every record after it, until another record sets it again. This saves repeating a long device name or a time in every record.","title":"Base field"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/glossary/#Resolving","page":"Words you'll see","text":"Turning records with base fields and relative times into self-contained ones: a full name, a unit, an absolute time, and the base value or sum added in (RFC 8428 section 4.6). resolve gives ResolvedRecords.","title":"Resolving"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/glossary/#Relative-time","page":"Words you'll see","text":"A time below 2^28 seconds (about 8.5 years) is counted from \"now\", the moment the pack is read. Larger times are seconds since 1970 (UTC).","title":"Relative time"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/glossary/#SenSML","page":"Words you'll see","text":"Sensor Measurement Streams: the same records, sent as a stream that may never end (section 4.8). The reader uses each record as soon as it arrives.","title":"SenSML"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/glossary/#Media-type","page":"Words you'll see","text":"The name that tells the receiver which format the bytes are in, such as application/senml+json. SenML has eight: four formats, each as SenML and SenSML. In SenMLFormat it is a MediaType.","title":"Media type"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/glossary/#Content-Format","page":"Words you'll see","text":"The number CoAP uses instead of a media type name. application/senml+cbor is\n\n","title":"Content-Format"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/glossary/#EXI","page":"Words you'll see","text":"Efficient XML Interchange: a W3C standard that sends XML as compact binary. SenML EXI is SenML's XML form coded with the schema of section 8. SenMLFormat uses EXIFormat for it.","title":"EXI"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/glossary/#Extension","page":"Words you'll see","text":"A label the RFC does not define. Readers keep it if they can and otherwise ignore it. A label that ends in _ must be understood, so a reader that doesn't know it refuses the pack.\n\n","title":"Extension"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/errors/#When-things-go-wrong","page":"When things go wrong","text":"In this chapter you'll see what the errors look like.\n\nEverything that can go wrong throws one exception type, SenMLError, in every format:\n\ninputs = [\n    \"cut off\" => \"\"\"[{\"n\":\"urn:dev:mac:0024befffe804ff1:room\",\"v\":21\"\"\",\n    \"two values\" => \"\"\"[{\"n\":\"urn:dev:mac:0024befffe804ff1:room\",\"v\":21,\"vb\":true}]\"\"\",\n    \"bad name\" => \"\"\"[{\"n\":\"room temp\",\"v\":21}]\"\"\",\n    \"newer version\" => \"\"\"[{\"bver\":11,\"n\":\"urn:dev:mac:0024befffe804ff1:room\",\"v\":21}]\"\"\",\n]\nfor (what, input) in inputs\n    try\n        pack = SenMLFormat.Json.decode(input)\n        println(what, \": ok, \", length(pack), \" records\")\n    catch e\n        e isa SenMLError || rethrow()\n        println(what, \": \", sprint(showerror, e))\n        println(\"    kind = \", e.kind)\n    end\nend\ncut off: SenMLError: JSON input ends inside the pack\n    kind = Incomplete\ntwo values: SenMLError: record 1: more than one of v, vs, vb, vd\n    kind = MultipleValues\nbad name: SenMLError: record 1: name must be [A-Za-z0-9][A-Za-z0-9:./_-]* (RFC 8428 4.5.1)\n    kind = InvalidName\nnewer version: SenMLError: record 1: SenML version 11 is newer than 10 and must not be used\n    kind = UnsupportedVersion\n\nThe message is for people; check kind in code. Each kind is named after rsenml's matching Error variant, so the two libraries can be checked against each other. Errors about one record say which one in record, counted from 1.\n\nkind Meaning\n:Syntax The bytes are not valid JSON, CBOR, XML or EXI. offset gives the byte.\n:Incomplete The input ended too early. For a stream: wait for more bytes.\n:WrongType, :DuplicateLabel, :MultipleValues A record's fields are wrong.\n:MustUnderstand A label ending in _ that SenMLFormat doesn't know.\n:InvalidLabel An extension uses a name the format keeps for a registered label.\n:InvalidBase64, :NonFinite A data value or a number that SenML can't carry.\n:EmptyPack, :MissingValue, :MissingName, :InvalidName The pack breaks a rule of section 4.\n:InvalidVersion, :UnsupportedVersion, :InconsistentVersion bver problems.\n:UnregisteredUnit Only with registered_units = true.\n:RelativeTime Resolving found a relative time, and no now was given.\n:Structure The XML or EXI root or record element is missing or wrong.\n:SchemaId The EXI stream doesn't name the RFC 8428 schema (\"a\").\n:Unrepresentable The pack can't be written in this format.\n:Exi An error from the EXI codec that has no SenML meaning.\n\n","title":"When things go wrong"},{"category":"section","location":"api/errors/#Errors","page":"Errors","text":"","title":"Errors"},{"category":"type","location":"api/errors/#SenMLFormat.SenMLError","page":"Errors","text":"SenMLError <: Exception\n\nEverything that can go wrong reading, checking, resolving or writing SenML. kind names what went wrong; the names are those of rsenml's Error variants:\n\nkind Meaning\n:Syntax the input is not well-formed JSON, CBOR, XML or EXI (format, offset, reason)\n:Incomplete the input ended inside the pack (format); for a SenSML stream: wait for more bytes\n:WrongType a registered label has a value of the wrong type (record, label)\n:DuplicateLabel a label occurs twice in one record (record, label)\n:MultipleValues more than one of v, vs, vb, vd (record)\n:MustUnderstand an unknown label ending in _ (record, label)\n:InvalidLabel an extension uses the name of a registered label (record, label)\n:InvalidBase64 vd is not unpadded base64url (record)\n:NonFinite a number is infinite or NaN (record, label)\n:EmptyPack a pack needs at least one record\n:MissingValue a record with regular fields but neither a value nor a sum (record)\n:MissingName a measurement with no name and no base name (record)\n:InvalidName base name + name breaks the rule of RFC 8428 section 4.5.1 (record)\n:InvalidVersion bver is not a positive integer (record)\n:UnsupportedVersion bver is newer than 10 (record, version)\n:InconsistentVersion records with different versions (record)\n:UnregisteredUnit a unit outside the registry, when registered units are required (record)\n:RelativeTime a relative time and no \"now\" to resolve it (record)\n:Structure the XML or EXI root or record element is wrong (format, reason)\n:SchemaId the EXI stream does not name schemaId \"a\" (found)\n:Unrepresentable the value cannot be written in this representation (format, reason)\n:Exi an EXI error with no SenML meaning (reason)\n\nrecord is the 1-based position of the record in the pack (0 when it does not apply). offset is a 0-based byte offset into the input (-1 when it does not apply); EXI works in bits, so it gives the byte that holds the bit. format is :json, :cbor, :xml, :exi, or :none.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SenMLFormat.SenMLError"},{"category":"section","location":"appendix/performance/#SenMLFormat-and-rsenml:-performance","page":"SenMLFormat and rsenml: performance","text":"Written by bench/run.sh; do not edit by hand.\n\nSenMLFormat is compared with rsenml only. Both read the same JSON files (bench/data): the RFC 8428 section 5.1.3 example and the 200-record synthetic pack of rsenml's compare (resolved records: three numeric sensors and a door switch, 30 s apart). Both are timed the same way (median_ns of rsenml's compare/src/perf.rs): calls batched to at least 200 µs, the median of 51 batches after 20 warm-up calls. bench/run.sh runs both three times, taking turns, and keeps the fastest median of each cell. Decoders validate and encoders validate first, as decode and encode do. \"decode+resolve\" is JSON decode, then resolution with a given \"now\".\n\nCPU: AMD Ryzen 7 7840U w/ Radeon 780M Graphics\nJulia: 1.12.7 (default flags)\nrsenml: v0.9.1, rustc 1.99.0 (b940084d7 2026-09-28), release profile with LTO, one codegen unit","title":"SenMLFormat and rsenml: performance"},{"category":"section","location":"appendix/performance/#Sizes-in-bytes","page":"SenMLFormat and rsenml: performance","text":"Codec RFC 5.1.3 (22 records) synthetic 200\nJSON 401 (equal) 14423 (equal)\nCBOR 245 (equal) 10446 (equal)\nXML 510 (equal) 14976 (equal)\nEXI bit-packed 161 (equal) 2486 (equal)\nEXI byte-aligned 251 (equal) 4146 (equal)","title":"Sizes in bytes"},{"category":"section","location":"appendix/performance/#RFC-5.1.3-(22-records):-time-per-call-in-µs","page":"SenMLFormat and rsenml: performance","text":"Codec Operation rsenml SenMLFormat SenMLFormat / rsenml\nJSON decode 2.5 9.5 3.9×\nJSON encode 2.0 3.9 2.0×\nJSON decode+resolve 3.3 8.5 2.6×\nCBOR decode 1.6 3.6 2.3×\nCBOR encode 1.0 1.7 1.7×\nXML decode 3.7 11.2 3.1×\nXML encode 1.8 4.0 2.2×\nEXI bit-packed decode 4.8 15.9 3.3×\nEXI bit-packed encode 3.3 5.9 1.8×\nEXI byte-aligned decode 5.0 16.1 3.2×\nEXI byte-aligned encode 3.5 6.2 1.8×","title":"RFC 5.1.3 (22 records): time per call in µs"},{"category":"section","location":"appendix/performance/#synthetic-200:-time-per-call-in-µs","page":"SenMLFormat and rsenml: performance","text":"Codec Operation rsenml SenMLFormat SenMLFormat / rsenml\nJSON decode 60.0 115.8 1.9×\nJSON encode 35.5 72.0 2.0×\nJSON decode+resolve 77.9 157.1 2.0×\nCBOR decode 28.0 59.0 2.1×\nCBOR encode 15.7 33.2 2.1×\nXML decode 63.5 190.4 3.0×\nXML encode 43.0 109.1 2.5×\nEXI bit-packed decode 78.4 245.6 3.1×\nEXI bit-packed encode 43.6 98.0 2.2×\nEXI byte-aligned decode 78.2 242.6 3.1×\nEXI byte-aligned encode 41.7 92.7 2.2×\n\n","title":"synthetic 200: time per call in µs"},{"category":"section","location":"api/formats/#Formats","page":"Formats","text":"Each format is a module with the same shape: decode and encode for whole packs (both validate; the _unchecked versions don't), RecordReader to read record by record, and StreamEncoder to write record by record. JSON and CBOR also have a StreamDecoder that takes bytes as they arrive.","title":"Formats"},{"category":"section","location":"api/formats/#By-media-type","page":"Formats","text":"","title":"By media type"},{"category":"section","location":"api/formats/#JSON","page":"Formats","text":"","title":"JSON"},{"category":"section","location":"api/formats/#CBOR","page":"Formats","text":"","title":"CBOR"},{"category":"section","location":"api/formats/#XML","page":"Formats","text":"","title":"XML"},{"category":"section","location":"api/formats/#EXI","page":"Formats","text":"","title":"EXI"},{"category":"section","location":"api/formats/#Reading-and-writing-streams","page":"Formats","text":"Each format module has its own next_record!, record! and finish!, with the same meaning in all four. finish(reader) (finish) checks the end of the input.\n\n","title":"Reading and writing streams"},{"category":"function","location":"api/formats/#SenMLFormat.decode","page":"Formats","text":"decode(media::MediaType, input) -> Pack\n\nReads and checks a pack of the given media type. input is bytes or a string. The SenSML types also accept streams; for CBOR that means an indefinite-length array. For EXI, the header must carry its options (RFC 8428 section 8); use SenMLFormat.Exi.decode with out_of_band for streams without them.\n\npack = SenMLFormat.decode(SENML_CBOR, bytes)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SenMLFormat.decode"},{"category":"function","location":"api/formats/#SenMLFormat.encode","page":"Formats","text":"encode(media::MediaType, pack) -> Vector{UInt8}\n\nChecks a pack and writes it in the given media type. SenSML CBOR uses an indefinite-length array; the other SenSML types are written the same as SenML. EXI is bit-packed with no cookie; SenMLFormat.Exi.encode has the other choices.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SenMLFormat.encode"},{"category":"function","location":"api/formats/#SenMLFormat.encode_unchecked","page":"Formats","text":"encode_unchecked(media::MediaType, pack) -> Vector{UInt8}\n\nencode without checking the pack first, for packs that are already valid (decoded, or built by trusted code). Values a format cannot write are still refused.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SenMLFormat.encode_unchecked"},{"category":"module","location":"api/formats/#SenMLFormat.Json","page":"Formats","text":"SenMLFormat.Json\n\nThe JSON format of SenML (RFC 8428 section 5): media types application/senml+json and, for streams, application/sensml+json.\n\nThe first example of RFC 8428 section 5.1.2:\n\npack = SenMLFormat.Json.decode(\"\"\"[{\"bn\":\"urn:dev:ow:10e2073a01080063:\",\"n\":\"voltage\",\"u\":\"V\",\"v\":120.1},\n                               {\"n\":\"current\",\"u\":\"A\",\"v\":1.2}]\"\"\")\nSenMLFormat.Json.encode(pack)  # the same JSON, without whitespace\n\nReading checks the JSON grammar, label types, repeated labels, at most one value per record, and labels ending in _. decode then validates the pack. Unknown labels with string, number or boolean values are kept as Extensions; other unknown labels are skipped.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SenMLFormat.Json"},{"category":"function","location":"api/formats/#SenMLFormat.Json.decode","page":"Formats","text":"decode(input) -> Pack\n\nReads a complete SenML JSON pack (bytes or a string) and validates it (RFC 8428 section 4). Throws a SenMLError.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SenMLFormat.Json.decode"},{"category":"function","location":"api/formats/#SenMLFormat.Json.decode_unchecked","page":"Formats","text":"decode_unchecked(input) -> Pack\n\nReads a complete SenML JSON pack (bytes or a string), checking only the JSON form: grammar, label types, repeated labels, at most one value, labels ending in _. Use validate for the rules of section 4.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SenMLFormat.Json.decode_unchecked"},{"category":"function","location":"api/formats/#SenMLFormat.Json.encode","page":"Formats","text":"encode(pack) -> Vector{UInt8}\n\nValidates a pack (RFC 8428 section 4) and writes it as SenML JSON.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SenMLFormat.Json.encode"},{"category":"function","location":"api/formats/#SenMLFormat.Json.encode_unchecked","page":"Formats","text":"encode_unchecked(pack) -> Vector{UInt8}\n\nWrites a pack as it is, without validating. Infinite and NaN numbers are still refused, because JSON has no way to write them.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SenMLFormat.Json.encode_unchecked"},{"category":"function","location":"api/formats/#SenMLFormat.Json.encode_record","page":"Formats","text":"encode_record(io, record, index)\n\nWrites one record object (for writing a SenSML stream one piece at a time; see StreamEncoder). index (1-based) is only used in error reports.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SenMLFormat.Json.encode_record"},{"category":"type","location":"api/formats/#SenMLFormat.Json.RecordReader","page":"Formats","text":"RecordReader(input)\n\nReads the records of a complete SenML JSON pack one at a time, without collecting them. Records are checked as they are read (JSON form only); feed them to a Validator for the rules of section 4. Iterate it, or call next_record! until it returns nothing, then finish.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SenMLFormat.Json.RecordReader"},{"category":"type","location":"api/formats/#SenMLFormat.Json.StreamEncoder","page":"Formats","text":"StreamEncoder()\n\nWrites a SenSML JSON stream one piece at a time: record! writes [ before the first record and , before the others; finish! writes ] (or [] if no record was written). It does not validate; run a Validator next to it if needed.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SenMLFormat.Json.StreamEncoder"},{"category":"type","location":"api/formats/#SenMLFormat.Json.StreamDecoder","page":"Formats","text":"StreamDecoder()\nStreamDecoder(; registered_units = false)\n\nDecodes a SenSML JSON stream (section 4.8) as its bytes arrive. push! bytes, then call next_record! until it returns nothing; each record is ready (and validated) as soon as its closing brace has arrived. isfinished tells whether the closing ] was read; finish ends the stream.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SenMLFormat.Json.StreamDecoder"},{"category":"module","location":"api/formats/#SenMLFormat.Cbor","page":"Formats","text":"SenMLFormat.Cbor\n\nThe CBOR format of SenML (RFC 8428 section 6): media types application/senml+cbor and, for streams, application/sensml+cbor. CBOR itself is RFC 8949.\n\nThe example of RFC 8428 section 5.1.1:\n\npack = Pack([Record(n = \"urn:dev:ow:10e2073a01080063\", u = \"Cel\", v = 23.1)])\nbytes = SenMLFormat.Cbor.encode(pack)\nSenMLFormat.Cbor.decode(bytes) == pack\n\nReading:\n\nRegistered labels are the integer keys of Table 4. A text key that spells a registered label (\"v\") is not that label in CBOR, so it is an unknown field and is ignored (section 4.4); its value is skipped, not kept, so a translation to JSON cannot write the label twice. Unknown integer keys are ignored. Unknown text keys are extensions (kept when their value is a scalar), and fail when they end in _.\nNumbers may be integers, half/single/double floats, or decimal fractions (tag 4). bver may only be an unsigned integer. Text and byte strings must have a definite length.\nA SenML pack is a definite-length array. A SenSML stream may also be an indefinite-length one (decode_sensml).\n\nWriting puts every number in the smallest form that reads back as the same double (an integer when it is a whole number, else a half, single or double float), the integer keys in the order of the RFC's examples, then extensions under text keys. This writes the RFC's section 6 example byte for byte.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SenMLFormat.Cbor"},{"category":"function","location":"api/formats/#SenMLFormat.Cbor.decode","page":"Formats","text":"decode(bytes) -> Pack\n\nReads a complete SenML CBOR pack (a definite-length array) and validates it. Throws a SenMLError.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SenMLFormat.Cbor.decode"},{"category":"function","location":"api/formats/#SenMLFormat.Cbor.decode_sensml","page":"Formats","text":"decode_sensml(bytes) -> Pack\n\nReads a complete SenSML CBOR stream, whose array may have an indefinite length (section 6), and validates it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SenMLFormat.Cbor.decode_sensml"},{"category":"function","location":"api/formats/#SenMLFormat.Cbor.decode_unchecked","page":"Formats","text":"decode_unchecked(bytes) -> Pack\n\nReads a complete SenML CBOR pack (a definite-length array), checking only the CBOR form. Use validate for the rules of section 4.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SenMLFormat.Cbor.decode_unchecked"},{"category":"function","location":"api/formats/#SenMLFormat.Cbor.encode","page":"Formats","text":"encode(pack) -> Vector{UInt8}\n\nValidates a pack and writes it as SenML CBOR (a definite-length array).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SenMLFormat.Cbor.encode"},{"category":"function","location":"api/formats/#SenMLFormat.Cbor.encode_sensml","page":"Formats","text":"encode_sensml(pack) -> Vector{UInt8}\n\nValidates a pack and writes it as a SenSML stream: an indefinite-length array.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SenMLFormat.Cbor.encode_sensml"},{"category":"function","location":"api/formats/#SenMLFormat.Cbor.encode_unchecked","page":"Formats","text":"encode_unchecked(pack) -> Vector{UInt8}\n\nWrites a pack (a definite-length array) as it is, without validating (infinite and NaN numbers are still refused).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SenMLFormat.Cbor.encode_unchecked"},{"category":"function","location":"api/formats/#SenMLFormat.Cbor.encode_record","page":"Formats","text":"encode_record(io, record, index)\n\nWrites one record map (a definite-length map). index (1-based) is only used in error reports.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SenMLFormat.Cbor.encode_record"},{"category":"type","location":"api/formats/#SenMLFormat.Cbor.RecordReader","page":"Formats","text":"RecordReader(input; sensml = false)\n\nReads the records of a complete SenML CBOR pack one at a time. With sensml, the array may also have an indefinite length (a SenSML stream). Iterate it, or call next_record! until it returns nothing, then finish.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SenMLFormat.Cbor.RecordReader"},{"category":"type","location":"api/formats/#SenMLFormat.Cbor.StreamEncoder","page":"Formats","text":"StreamEncoder()\n\nWrites a SenSML CBOR stream one piece at a time: the start of an indefinite-length array (0x9f) before the first record (record!), one map per record, then the break byte 0xff (finish!). It does not validate.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SenMLFormat.Cbor.StreamEncoder"},{"category":"type","location":"api/formats/#SenMLFormat.Cbor.StreamDecoder","page":"Formats","text":"StreamDecoder(; registered_units = false)\n\nDecodes a SenSML CBOR stream (section 4.8) as its bytes arrive. push! bytes, then call next_record! until it returns nothing; each record is ready (and validated) once all of its bytes are in.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SenMLFormat.Cbor.StreamDecoder"},{"category":"module","location":"api/formats/#SenMLFormat.Xml","page":"Formats","text":"SenMLFormat.Xml\n\nThe XML format of SenML (RFC 8428 section 7): media types application/senml+xml and application/sensml+xml.\n\nThe document is a sensml element in the namespace urn:ietf:params:xml:ns:senml, holding one senml element per record. Their attributes without a prefix are the fields (Table 5).\n\nReading:\n\nOnly UTF-8 is accepted (section 7). A DTD is refused (so no entity expansion).\nWhitespace, comments and processing instructions may appear between records. Other text and child elements may not.\nAttributes in other namespaces (xml:lang, xmlns:*, foreign ones) are ignored. Unknown attributes without a prefix are extensions (string values).\nINF, -INF and NaN are valid xs:double values but not SenML numbers.\n\nWriting gives <sensml xmlns=\"urn:ietf:params:xml:ns:senml\"> with one empty <senml .../> element per record, attributes in the order of the RFC's examples, no XML declaration and no whitespace.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SenMLFormat.Xml"},{"category":"function","location":"api/formats/#SenMLFormat.Xml.decode","page":"Formats","text":"decode(input) -> Pack\n\nReads a SenML XML document (bytes or a string) and validates the pack. Throws a SenMLError.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SenMLFormat.Xml.decode"},{"category":"function","location":"api/formats/#SenMLFormat.Xml.decode_unchecked","page":"Formats","text":"decode_unchecked(input) -> Pack\n\nReads a SenML XML document (bytes or a string), checking only the XML form (not the rules of section 4).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SenMLFormat.Xml.decode_unchecked"},{"category":"function","location":"api/formats/#SenMLFormat.Xml.encode","page":"Formats","text":"encode(pack) -> Vector{UInt8}\n\nValidates a pack and writes it as SenML XML.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SenMLFormat.Xml.encode"},{"category":"function","location":"api/formats/#SenMLFormat.Xml.encode_unchecked","page":"Formats","text":"encode_unchecked(pack) -> Vector{UInt8}\n\nWrites a pack as it is, without validating (values XML cannot carry are still refused).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SenMLFormat.Xml.encode_unchecked"},{"category":"function","location":"api/formats/#SenMLFormat.Xml.encode_record","page":"Formats","text":"encode_record(io, record, index)\n\nWrites one <senml .../> element (for writing a document one piece at a time; see StreamEncoder). index (1-based) is only used in error reports.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SenMLFormat.Xml.encode_record"},{"category":"type","location":"api/formats/#SenMLFormat.Xml.RecordReader","page":"Formats","text":"RecordReader(input)\n\nReads the records of a SenML XML document (bytes or a string) one at a time. Throws a SenMLError if the input is not UTF-8. Call next_record! until it returns nothing, then finish; or iterate it.\n\nRecords have no child elements, so namespaces only need two levels: the root's declarations (kept here) and each senml element's own.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SenMLFormat.Xml.RecordReader"},{"category":"type","location":"api/formats/#SenMLFormat.Xml.StreamEncoder","page":"Formats","text":"StreamEncoder()\n\nWrites a SenSML XML document one piece at a time: the sensml start tag before the first record (record!), one senml element per record, and the end tag (finish!). It does not validate.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SenMLFormat.Xml.StreamEncoder"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/formats/#SenMLFormat.XML_NAMESPACE","page":"Formats","text":"The XML namespace of SenML (RFC 8428 section 12.4).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SenMLFormat.XML_NAMESPACE"},{"category":"module","location":"api/formats/#SenMLFormat.Exi","page":"Formats","text":"SenMLFormat.Exi\n\nThe EXI format of SenML (RFC 8428 section 8): media types application/senml-exi and application/sensml-exi. Strict schema-informed EXI with the RFC's schema, through EXIFormat.\n\nbytes = SenMLFormat.Exi.encode(pack)                              # bit-packed\nbytes = SenMLFormat.Exi.encode(pack; alignment = :byte_aligned)   # byte-aligned\nSenMLFormat.Exi.decode(bytes)\n\nThe header carries the EXI options with schemaId \"a\" (section 8); a stream that names another schema (or none) is refused with a :SchemaId error. A stream without options is read only when the caller gives them (out_of_band).\nStrict EXI cannot carry extensions: they are dropped (section 8: \"any extensions will be lost\"), or refused with extensions = :error.\nEXI cannot carry -0.0: an EXI Float's mantissa is an EXI Integer, which codes a negative number as its magnitude minus one (EXI 7.1.5), so there is no negative zero. -0.0 is written as 0.\nNo $EXI cookie by default (section 8 says SHOULD NOT over CoAP or HTTP).\n\nThe bytes equal rsenml's and EXIficient's for every test case.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SenMLFormat.Exi"},{"category":"function","location":"api/formats/#SenMLFormat.Exi.decode","page":"Formats","text":"decode(bytes; out_of_band = nothing) -> Pack\n\nReads a SenML EXI stream and validates the pack. The header must carry options with schemaId \"a\", unless out_of_band gives the alignment of a stream without options. Throws a SenMLError.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SenMLFormat.Exi.decode"},{"category":"function","location":"api/formats/#SenMLFormat.Exi.decode_unchecked","page":"Formats","text":"decode_unchecked(bytes; out_of_band = nothing) -> Pack\n\nReads a SenML EXI stream without the rules of section 4.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SenMLFormat.Exi.decode_unchecked"},{"category":"function","location":"api/formats/#SenMLFormat.Exi.encode","page":"Formats","text":"encode(pack; alignment = :bit_packed, cookie = false, extensions = :drop) -> Vector{UInt8}\n\nValidates a pack and writes it as SenML EXI: strict, with the options and schemaId \"a\" in the header. alignment is :bit_packed (the default) or :byte_aligned; cookie writes the $EXI cookie; extensions = :error refuses a pack with extensions instead of dropping them.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SenMLFormat.Exi.encode"},{"category":"function","location":"api/formats/#SenMLFormat.Exi.encode_unchecked","page":"Formats","text":"encode_unchecked(pack; alignment = :bit_packed, cookie = false, extensions = :drop) -> Vector{UInt8}\n\nWrites a pack as it is, without validating (values EXI cannot carry are still refused).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SenMLFormat.Exi.encode_unchecked"},{"category":"type","location":"api/formats/#SenMLFormat.Exi.RecordReader","page":"Formats","text":"RecordReader(bytes; out_of_band = nothing)\n\nReads the records of a SenML EXI stream one at a time: next_record! until it returns nothing, then finish. out_of_band gives the alignment (:bit_packed or :byte_aligned) of a stream whose header carries no options; such a stream is refused (:SchemaId) without it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SenMLFormat.Exi.RecordReader"},{"category":"type","location":"api/formats/#SenMLFormat.Exi.StreamEncoder","page":"Formats","text":"StreamEncoder(; alignment = :bit_packed, cookie = false, extensions = :drop)\n\nWrites a SenSML EXI stream record by record: record! each record, then finish! for the bytes. It does not validate. alignment is :bit_packed (the smallest) or :byte_aligned; cookie writes the $EXI cookie; extensions is :drop or :error.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SenMLFormat.Exi.StreamEncoder"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/formats/#SenMLFormat.EXI_SCHEMA_ID","page":"Formats","text":"The EXI schemaId of the RFC 8428 schema. The EXI header options must carry it (RFC 8428 section 8).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SenMLFormat.EXI_SCHEMA_ID"},{"category":"function","location":"api/formats/#SenMLFormat.Json.next_record!","page":"Formats","text":"next_record!(reader) -> Union{Record,Nothing}\n\nThe next record, or nothing after the end of the pack (for a stream decoder: also when more bytes are needed).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SenMLFormat.Json.next_record!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/formats/#SenMLFormat.Json.record!","page":"Formats","text":"record!(enc, io, record)\n\nWrites the next record of the stream.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SenMLFormat.Json.record!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/formats/#SenMLFormat.Json.finish!","page":"Formats","text":"finish!(enc, io)\n\nWrites the end of the stream.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SenMLFormat.Json.finish!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/formats/#SenMLFormat.Json.isfinished","page":"Formats","text":"The closing ] has been read.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SenMLFormat.Json.isfinished"},{"category":"function","location":"api/formats/#SenMLFormat.Json.position","page":"Formats","text":"Byte offset (0-based) after the last record read.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SenMLFormat.Json.position"},{"category":"function","location":"api/formats/#SenMLFormat.Cbor.next_record!","page":"Formats","text":"next_record!(reader) -> Union{Record,Nothing}\n\nThe next record, or nothing at the end of the array (for a stream decoder: also when more bytes are needed).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SenMLFormat.Cbor.next_record!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/formats/#SenMLFormat.Cbor.record!","page":"Formats","text":"record!(enc, io, record)\n\nWrites the next record (with the array header 0x9f before the first).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SenMLFormat.Cbor.record!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/formats/#SenMLFormat.Cbor.finish!","page":"Formats","text":"finish!(enc, io)\n\nWrites the break code 0xff (and the header if no record was written).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SenMLFormat.Cbor.finish!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/formats/#SenMLFormat.Cbor.isfinished","page":"Formats","text":"The end of the array (or its break code) has been read.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SenMLFormat.Cbor.isfinished"},{"category":"function","location":"api/formats/#SenMLFormat.Cbor.position","page":"Formats","text":"Byte offset (0-based) after the last record read.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SenMLFormat.Cbor.position"},{"category":"function","location":"api/formats/#SenMLFormat.Xml.next_record!","page":"Formats","text":"next_record!(reader) -> Union{Record,Nothing}\n\nThe next record, or nothing at </sensml>.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SenMLFormat.Xml.next_record!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/formats/#SenMLFormat.Xml.record!","page":"Formats","text":"record!(enc, io, record)\n\nWrites the next record (with the sensml start tag before the first).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SenMLFormat.Xml.record!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/formats/#SenMLFormat.Xml.finish!","page":"Formats","text":"finish!(enc, io)\n\nWrites the end tag (and the start tag if no record was written).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SenMLFormat.Xml.finish!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/formats/#SenMLFormat.Exi.next_record!","page":"Formats","text":"next_record!(reader) -> Union{Record,Nothing}\n\nThe next record, or nothing at the end of the sensml element.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SenMLFormat.Exi.next_record!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/formats/#SenMLFormat.Exi.record!","page":"Formats","text":"record!(enc, record)\n\nWrites the next record of the stream.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SenMLFormat.Exi.record!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/formats/#SenMLFormat.Exi.finish!","page":"Formats","text":"finish!(enc) -> Vector{UInt8}\n\nEnds the stream and returns its bytes.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SenMLFormat.Exi.finish!"},{"category":"section","location":"api/units/#Units","page":"Units","text":"","title":"Units"},{"category":"type","location":"api/units/#SenMLFormat.Unit","page":"Units","text":"Unit(symbol, description, deprecated)\n\nOne registered unit: the symbol used in u / bu (for example Cel), what it measures as Table 6 describes it, and whether Table 6 marks it * (new implementations are NOT RECOMMENDED to write it, but readers SHOULD understand it).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SenMLFormat.Unit"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/units/#SenMLFormat.UNITS","page":"Units","text":"RFC 8428 Table 6, in table order.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SenMLFormat.UNITS"},{"category":"function","location":"api/units/#SenMLFormat.lookup_unit","page":"Units","text":"lookup_unit(symbol) -> Union{Unit,Nothing}\n\nThe registered unit with this symbol (case-sensitive).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SenMLFormat.lookup_unit"},{"category":"section","location":"api/model/#Records-and-packs","page":"Records and packs","text":"The data model every format shares. A Pack is a vector of Records, each as written, base fields included.\n\n","title":"Records and packs"},{"category":"module","location":"api/model/#SenMLFormat","page":"Records and packs","text":"SenMLFormat\n\nSensor Measurement Lists (RFC 8428): records and packs, read and written as JSON, CBOR, XML and EXI. The Julia twin of the Rust library rsenml: same rules, same bytes.\n\nRecord, Pack, Extension: records as written, base fields included.\nvalidate / Validator: the rules of section 4.\nresolve / Resolver: records with the base fields applied (section 4.6).\nlookup_unit, UNITS: the units registry (section 12.1).\nRecordSelector: rec= fragment identifiers (section 9).\nMediaType: media types and CoAP Content-Formats (sections 12.3, 12.5).\nSenMLFormat.decode, SenMLFormat.encode, SenMLFormat.encode_unchecked: any format, chosen by media type. Not exported, as decode and encode are common names; call them as SenMLFormat.decode(SENML_JSON, bytes).\nSenMLFormat.Json, SenMLFormat.Cbor, SenMLFormat.Xml, SenMLFormat.Exi: the JSON (section 5), CBOR (section 6), XML (section 7) and EXI (section 8) formats, with their record readers, stream writers and options.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SenMLFormat"},{"category":"type","location":"api/model/#SenMLFormat.Record","page":"Records and packs","text":"Record(; bn, bt, bu, bv, bs, bver, n, u, v, vs, vb, vd, s, t, ut, extensions)\n\nOne SenML record as written, base fields included. Every field is optional (nothing when absent). Give at most one of v, vs, vb, vd (or value). extensions is a vector of Extensions or label => value pairs.\n\nRecord(bn = \"urn:dev:mac:0024befffe804ff1:\", n = \"supply-air\", u = \"Cel\", v = 12.8)\n\nFields: bn, bt, bu, bv, bs, bver, n, u, value, s, t, ut, extensions. Numbers are Float64, bver is UInt64, value is a Value.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SenMLFormat.Record"},{"category":"type","location":"api/model/#SenMLFormat.Pack","page":"Records and packs","text":"Pack(records)\nPack()\n\nA SenML pack (or a complete SenSML stream): the records in order. A Pack is an AbstractVector{Record}.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SenMLFormat.Pack"},{"category":"type","location":"api/model/#SenMLFormat.Value","page":"Records and packs","text":"Value\n\nThe value of a record (one of v, vs, vb, vd, RFC 8428 section 4.2): Float64 for v, String for vs, Bool for vb, Vector{UInt8} for vd.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SenMLFormat.Value"},{"category":"type","location":"api/model/#SenMLFormat.Extension","page":"Records and packs","text":"Extension(label, value)\n\nA field with a label RFC 8428 does not register (section 4.4).\n\nReaders must ignore unknown labels. SenMLFormat still keeps the ones whose value is a single scalar (number, string, Bool or bytes), which is what the CDDL of section 11 allows, so a pack converted from one format to another keeps them. JSON and XML cannot tell a string from base64 data, so an unknown label read from them is never bytes (and from XML always a string).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SenMLFormat.Extension"},{"category":"function","location":"api/model/#SenMLFormat.is_base_only","page":"Records and packs","text":"is_base_only(r::Record)\n\nA record with only base fields sets values for the records after it. It is not a measurement itself (see the first record of the example in section 5.1.7).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SenMLFormat.is_base_only"},{"category":"function","location":"api/model/#SenMLFormat.has_base_fields","page":"Records and packs","text":"True when the record carries any base field (registered or extension).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SenMLFormat.has_base_fields"},{"category":"function","location":"api/model/#SenMLFormat.has_regular_fields","page":"Records and packs","text":"has_regular_fields(r::Record)\n\nTrue when the record carries any regular (non-base) field of RFC 8428. Extensions do not count: a reader ignores labels it does not know (section 4.4), so a record of base fields plus an unknown label is still base-only.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SenMLFormat.has_regular_fields"},{"category":"function","location":"api/model/#SenMLFormat.base64url_encode","page":"Records and packs","text":"base64url_encode(data) -> String\n\ndata as base64url without padding.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SenMLFormat.base64url_encode"},{"category":"function","location":"api/model/#SenMLFormat.base64url_decode","page":"Records and packs","text":"base64url_decode(text) -> Union{Vector{UInt8},Nothing}\n\nDecodes base64url without padding. Returns nothing if text is not exactly that.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SenMLFormat.base64url_decode"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/model/#SenMLFormat.SUPPORTED_VERSION","page":"Records and packs","text":"The SenML version SenMLFormat implements, which is also the default when there is no bver (section 4.1).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SenMLFormat.SUPPORTED_VERSION"},{"category":"section","location":"#SenMLFormat.jl","page":"Home","text":"SenMLFormat reads and writes sensor readings in Julia. This manual shows you how, one short example at a time.\n\nThe format is SenML (Sensor Measurement Lists, RFC 8428). SenML is a small, standard way for a device to report what it measured: a list of records, each with a name, a value, and maybe a unit and a time. A thermostat, a water meter and a web app can all agree on it without sharing any code.\n\nThe same list can be sent in four formats. SenMLFormat reads and writes all of them from one data model:\n\nflowchart LR\n    pack[Pack of records] --> json[JSON text]\n    pack --> cbor[CBOR binary]\n    pack --> xml[XML text]\n    pack --> exi[EXI binary]\n    json --> back[The same pack]\n    cbor --> back\n    xml --> back\n    exi --> back\n\nSenMLFormat is the Julia version of rsenml. It writes the same bytes in every format and gives the same verdicts on the same test data.","title":"SenMLFormat.jl"},{"category":"section","location":"#Installation","page":"Home","text":"SenMLFormat needs Julia 1.10 or newer. In the Pkg REPL (press ]):\n\npkg> add SenMLFormat\n\nor with using Pkg; Pkg.add(\"SenMLFormat\"). It brings in EXIFormat for EXI.","title":"Installation"},{"category":"section","location":"#Quick-start","page":"Home","text":"using SenMLFormat\n\npack = Pack([\n    Record(bn = \"urn:dev:mac:0024befffe804ff1:\", bt = 1.7e9, n = \"supply-air\", u = \"Cel\", v = 12.8),\n    Record(n = \"return-air\", u = \"Cel\", v = 24.1),\n    Record(n = \"compressor\", vb = true),\n])\nfor media in (SENML_JSON, SENML_CBOR, SENML_XML, SENML_EXI)\n    bytes = SenMLFormat.encode(media, pack)\n    @assert SenMLFormat.decode(media, bytes) == pack\n    println(content_type(media), \": \", length(bytes), \" bytes\")\nend\napplication/senml+json: 159 bytes\napplication/senml+cbor: 110 bytes\napplication/senml+xml: 218 bytes\napplication/senml-exi: 87 bytes","title":"Quick start"},{"category":"section","location":"#What-you-will-learn","page":"Home","text":"Each chapter of the manual follows one small script in examples/basics, from easiest to hardest:\n\nYour first pack: build a pack, write it as JSON and read it back.\nChoosing a format: the eight media types, their sizes and CoAP numbers.\nBase fields and resolving: how records share names and times, and how to get full names and absolute times out.\nChecking packs: what the RFC's rules refuse, and the stricter option.\nStreaming with SenSML: writing and reading one record at a time.\nWhen things go wrong: the errors you can get back.\n\nEvery script has a Rust twin in rsenml's examples/basics with the same data.","title":"What you will learn"},{"category":"section","location":"#Where-to-go-next","page":"Home","text":"The Manual starts with Getting started.\nThe API reference documents every public name, starting with Records and packs.\nWhere the RFC is unclear lists the choices made where RFC 8428 leaves room.\n\n","title":"Where to go next"},{"category":"section","location":"appendix/thanks/#Thanks","page":"Thanks","text":"SenML is a small standard that does one job well, and SenMLFormat would not exist without the people who wrote it and the work it stands on.\n\nCullen Jennings, Zach Shelby, Jari Arkko, Ari Keränen and Carsten Bormann wrote RFC 8428. Its examples, hex dumps and schema are the first tests SenMLFormat must pass.\nDaniel Peintner wrote EXIficient, with Richard Kuntschke at Siemens. SenMLFormat's EXI bytes are checked against EXIficient's.\nToralf Wittner (minicbor), Yevhenii Reizner (xmlparser) and Gronis (nanojson) wrote the Rust parsers whose grammar rules SenMLFormat's own readers follow, so both libraries agree on every input.\n\nThank you.\n\n","title":"Thanks"},{"category":"section","location":"appendix/comparison/#SenMLFormat-and-rsenml-compared","page":"SenMLFormat and rsenml compared","text":"SenMLFormat is the Julia port of rsenml 0.9, and this compares only those two. Speed is in performance.md, which bench/run.sh writes.","title":"SenMLFormat and rsenml compared"},{"category":"section","location":"appendix/comparison/#Same-results","page":"SenMLFormat and rsenml compared","text":"SenMLFormat gives the same answers as rsenml, and the tests check it against rsenml's own output (test/fixtures/export, written by rsenml's scripts/export-fixtures.sh):\n\nthe same verdict (accepted, or the same error kind) on all 66 of rsenml's conformance vectors;\nthe same bytes in all eight media types and both EXI alignments for every test case and every accepted vector, and the same sizes on the benchmark data;\nthe same EXI bytes as EXIficient 1.0.7 on all 32 streams;\nthe same 25 conformance rows, with the same numbers (conformance.md);\nthe same 10 readings of unclear points of RFC 8428 (Where the RFC is unclear).","title":"Same results"},{"category":"section","location":"appendix/comparison/#Features","page":"SenMLFormat and rsenml compared","text":"Feature rsenml SenMLFormat\nJSON, CBOR, XML, EXI read and written yes yes\nAll 8 media types, CoAP Content-Formats yes yes\ndecode / encode / encode_unchecked by media type yes yes (SenMLFormat.decode, not exported)\nWhole-pack decode, decode_unchecked all formats all formats\nRecord-by-record RecordReader all formats all formats\nSenSML StreamDecoder (bytes as they arrive) JSON, CBOR JSON, CBOR\nStreamEncoder (a record at a time) all formats all formats\nValidation, with the registered-units option yes yes\nResolution (section 4.6) yes yes\nUnits registry, rec= fragments, base64url yes yes\nEXI options: alignment, cookie, drop or refuse extensions, out-of-band options yes yes\nExtensions kept through JSON and CBOR yes yes\nErrors Error enum with variants one SenMLError; kind is the variant's name\nWrite into a fixed buffer (encode_into) JSON, CBOR, XML, EXI no (not needed in Julia; the JSON, CBOR and XML writers take any IO)\nZero-copy decoding (strings borrowed from the input) CBOR, XML (Cow); JSON and EXI copy no (records own their strings)\nno_std, microcontroller builds, footprint measured yes (Cortex-M) not applicable\nOptional formats one Cargo feature per format all four always loaded\nRobustness testing cargo-fuzz targets for every decoder, plus mutation tests mutation tests (truncations and 64 random changes per input by default; set SENML_MUTATIONS for more); no coverage-guided fuzzing\nStatic checks clippy, forbid(unsafe_code) Aqua, JET\nPackage registry not on crates.io (GitHub tag) General registry (Pkg.add(\"SenMLFormat\"))\nMinimum toolchain Rust 1.88 Julia 1.10\nDependencies nanojson, minicbor, xmlparser, rexi EXIFormat, PrecompileTools (own JSON, CBOR and XML readers)\nExample CoAP todo list with clients in four languages examples/basics","title":"Features"},{"category":"section","location":"appendix/comparison/#Speed-in-short","page":"SenMLFormat and rsenml compared","text":"See performance.md for the tables. Measured on an AMD Ryzen 7 7840U with Julia 1.12.7 and rsenml v0.9.1:\n\nOperation SenMLFormat / rsenml\ndecode (all formats) about 2× to 4× slower\nencode (all formats) about 1.7× to 2.5× slower\n\n","title":"Speed in short"},{"category":"section","location":"appendix/side-by-side/#rsenml-and-SenMLFormat-side-by-side","page":"rsenml and SenMLFormat side by side","text":"This page shows how each rsenml call looks in SenMLFormat, so you can move between Rust and Julia.\n\nrsenml is the Rust version of SenMLFormat. SenMLFormat takes its test vectors from rsenml's fixtures/export, writes the same bytes in every format and gives the same verdicts. Its manual follows rsenml's guide chapter for chapter, with the same devices and data.\n\nTask SenMLFormat (Julia) rsenml (Rust)\nBuild a record Record(n = \"room\", v = 21.5) Record::new().with_name(\"room\").with_value(21.5)\nBuild a pack Pack([r1, r2]) [r1, r2].into_iter().collect::<Pack>()\nWrite JSON SenMLFormat.Json.encode(pack) rsenml::json::encode(&pack)?\nRead CBOR SenMLFormat.Cbor.decode(bytes) rsenml::cbor::decode(&bytes)?\nByte-aligned EXI SenMLFormat.Exi.encode(pack; alignment = :byte_aligned) exi::encode_with(&pack, &EncodeOptions::new().with_alignment(Alignment::ByteAligned))\nBy media type SenMLFormat.encode(SENML_CBOR, pack) rsenml::encode(MediaType::SenmlCbor, &pack)\nCheck validate(pack) pack.validate()\nResolve resolve(pack; now) pack.resolve(Some(now))\nErrors SenMLError, with kind (:MissingValue, ...) Result with rsenml::Error (Error::MissingValue, ...)\nRecord numbers in errors from 1 from 0\n\nOnly in rsenml: no_std and fixed buffers (encode_into).\n\n","title":"rsenml and SenMLFormat side by side"},{"category":"section","location":"appendix/conformance/#RFC-8428-conformance-checklist","page":"RFC 8428 conformance checklist","text":"Each requirement, where SenMLFormat implements it, and the test that shows it. The row numbers are those of rsenml's conformance checklist. Tests are named file / testset. \"All formats\" means JSON, CBOR and XML read the same way through one RecordBuilder (EXI's strict grammar cannot express most violations in the first place).\n\n# Requirement RFC Implementation Tests\n1 All labels of Tables 1, 2, 4, 5 with their types 4.3, 5, 6, 7 src/core/label.jl core.jl / labels; exi.jl / every field and value type round trips; all-fields case in shared_vectors.jl\n2 CBOR integer keys exactly as Table 4 6 src/core/label.jl (CBOR keys) cbor.jl / RFC 8428 section 6 both ways\n3 Unknown labels ignored (kept as extensions) 4.4 RecordBuilder (src/core/builder.jl) json.jl / unknown labels are kept or skipped; cbor.jl / unknown integer keys are ignored and text extensions kept\n3a CBOR text key spelling a registered label (\"v\") is an unrecognized field: ignored, value dropped 4.4, 6, 12.2; RFC 9193 8 src/cbor.jl cbor.jl / text keys spelling registered labels are ignored\n4 Unknown label ending in _ is an error 4.4 RecordBuilder json.jl, cbor.jl / record rules; xml.jl / XSD types\n5 Version: positive integer, same in every record, newer than 10 refused; older accepted 4.1, 4.4; RFC 9100 2-3 Validator (src/core/validate.jl) core.jl / validation; json.jl / record rules\n6 Exactly one value unless a sum is present 4.2 RecordBuilder, Validator core.jl / builder, validation\n7 Name optional when a base name is present 4.2 Validator core.jl / validation\n8 Concatenated name character set and first character 4.5.1 valid_name core.jl / validation, name rule matches the concatenation check\n9 Unit falls back to base unit 4.5.2 Resolver (src/core/resolve.jl) core.jl / resolution; rfc_examples.jl / 5.1.4 is 5.1.3 resolved\n10 Time = bt + t; below 2^28 relative to now, else absolute 4.5.3 Resolver, ABSOLUTE_TIME_THRESHOLD rfc_examples.jl / 5.1.7 actuation times\n11 Sum = bs + s when either present; value = bv + v 4.5.4 Resolver core.jl / resolution\n12 Resolved records: no base fields, no relative times, chronological, bver only when not 10 4.6 resolve, to_record rfc_examples.jl / 5.1.4 is 5.1.3 resolved\n13 Base values apply until the next record with the same base field 4 Resolver rfc_examples.jl / 5.1.6 mixes base names\n14 SenSML: records usable before the stream ends 4.8 Json.StreamDecoder, Cbor.StreamDecoder rfc_examples.jl / the SenSML example streams record by record; json.jl, cbor.jl / stream decoder takes bytes as they come\n15 JSON: UTF-8, RFC 8259 escapes, IEEE double range, lower-case e 5 src/json.jl; write_number (src/core/number.jl) json.jl / escapes in strings and keys, JSON grammar is enforced, numbers keep their value in shortest form; core.jl / numbers\n16 Data Value: unpadded base64url in JSON and XML, byte string in CBOR 4.3, 5, 6, 7 src/core/base64url.jl; CBOR byte strings core.jl / base64url; json.jl / record rules; xml.jl / XSD types; cbor.jl / record rules\n17 CBOR numbers convert back to the same double; bver unsigned only 6 write_number, CBOR reader cbor.jl / numbers take the smallest exact form, every numeric form is read, record rules\n18 CBOR: definite-length strings; definite array for SenML, indefinite for SenSML 6 src/cbor.jl cbor.jl / SenSML uses an indefinite array, record rules; facade.jl / SenSML CBOR is an indefinite array\n19 XML: UTF-8, namespace urn:ietf:params:xml:ns:senml, sensml/senml elements, Table 5 attributes 7 src/xml.jl xml.jl / RFC 8428 section 7, namespaces, prefixes and foreign attributes, structure is enforced\n20 EXI: strict schema mode with the RFC's XSD 8 src/exi.jl, the RFC 8428 section 8 XSD, scripts/gen-tables.sh exi.jl / RFC 8428 section 8 both ways; the gate checks the generated tables\n21 EXI header includes options with schemaId \"a\"; no cookie by default 8 src/exi.jl exi.jl / schemaId is checked, cookie\n22 EXI bit-packed and byte-aligned 8 alignment keyword exi.jl / RFC 8428 section 8 both ways\n23 Units registry 12.1 src/core/units.jl (Table 6), Validator(registered_units = true) core.jl / units, validation\n24 rec= fragment identifiers 9 RecordSelector (src/core/fragment.jl) core.jl / fragments\n25 Media types and CoAP Content-Formats 12.3, 12.5 MediaType (src/core/media.jl), SenMLFormat.decode/encode core.jl / media types; facade.jl","title":"RFC 8428 conformance checklist"},{"category":"section","location":"appendix/conformance/#The-RFC's-examples","page":"RFC 8428 conformance checklist","text":"All extracted at test time (test/helpers.jl) from the RFC 8428 text that scripts/fetch-standards.sh downloads:\n\nVector Result\nJSON examples 5.1.1 - 5.1.7 (11 arrays) all valid; 5.1.2's SenSML stream yields its records before the stream closes\n5.1.4 equals 5.1.3 resolved\nCBOR hex dump, section 6 decoded = 5.1.2 (with its explicit t: 0); encoded byte for byte\nXML, section 7 decoded = 5.1.2\nEXI hex dump, section 8, bit-packed (63 bytes) decoded = its XML; encoded byte for byte\nEXI hex dump, section 8, byte-aligned decoded = its XML; encoded byte for byte; the hand-coded sensor edit works\nTable 3, EXI size of 5.1.3 161 bytes, as in the RFC","title":"The RFC's examples"},{"category":"section","location":"appendix/conformance/#Beyond-the-RFC's-examples","page":"RFC 8428 conformance checklist","text":"Same verdicts as rsenml. All 66 of rsenml's conformance vectors (JSON, CBOR, SenSML CBOR; exported by rsenml's scripts/export-fixtures.sh) get rsenml's verdict, and the accepted ones the expected records (shared_vectors.jl).\nSame bytes as rsenml. For every accepted vector and all 16 test cases, JSON, CBOR, SenSML CBOR, XML and both EXI alignments equal rsenml's bytes (shared_vectors.jl); every case in all eight media types through SenMLFormat.encode, SenMLFormat.encode_unchecked and SenMLFormat.decode (facade.jl).\nSame bytes as EXIficient. All 32 EXIficient 1.0.7 streams (16 cases, two alignments), byte for byte (exi.jl).\nEvery case round-trips through every format, and JSON -> CBOR -> XML -> EXI -> JSON gives the original JSON (cbor.jl / cross format).\nOnly SenMLError escapes any decoder, on every truncation and on 64 random changes per input (SENML_MUTATIONS=2000 for long runs) of every case in every format, fed to every decoder and both stream decoders; nor on deep nesting, oversized CBOR lengths or an XML entity bomb (robustness.jl, json.jl, cbor.jl, xml.jl / hostile input, entity expansion is refused).\n\n","title":"Beyond the RFC's examples"},{"category":"section","location":"api/validate/#Validation","page":"Validation","text":"","title":"Validation"},{"category":"function","location":"api/validate/#SenMLFormat.validate","page":"Validation","text":"validate(pack; registered_units = false)\n\nChecks the rules of RFC 8428 section 4 (see Validator). Throws a SenMLError; returns nothing.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SenMLFormat.validate"},{"category":"type","location":"api/validate/#SenMLFormat.Validator","page":"Validation","text":"Validator(; registered_units = false)\n\nChecks the records of a pack one at a time, so a SenSML stream can be checked as its records arrive: check! each record, then finish.\n\nWith registered_units, units (u, bu) must be in the SenML Units registry (Table 6, section 12.1). RFC 8428 does not require it, so it is off by default.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SenMLFormat.Validator"},{"category":"function","location":"api/validate/#SenMLFormat.check!","page":"Validation","text":"check!(v::Validator, record)\n\nChecks the next record of the pack. Throws a SenMLError.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SenMLFormat.check!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/validate/#SenMLFormat.finish","page":"Validation","text":"finish(reader)\n\nChecks that only comments, processing instructions and whitespace follow the root element.\n\n\n\n\n\nChecks that the array ended and nothing follows it.\n\n\n\n\n\nfinish(d::StreamDecoder)\n\nEnds the stream. It must be closed, hold at least one record, and have nothing after it.\n\n\n\n\n\nfinish(reader)\n\nChecks that the pack was closed and only whitespace follows.\n\n\n\n\n\nfinish(d::StreamDecoder)\n\nEnds the stream. It must be closed, hold at least one record, and have only whitespace after it.\n\n\n\n\n\nfinish(reader)\n\nChecks that the stream ends after the document.\n\n\n\n\n\nfinish(v::Validator)\n\nEnds the pack. It must have held at least one record (the CDDL is [1* record], section 11).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SenMLFormat.finish"},{"category":"function","location":"api/validate/#SenMLFormat.valid_name","page":"Validation","text":"valid_name(base_name, name)\n\nSection 4.5.1: the joined name base_name * name must match [A-Za-z0-9][A-Za-z0-9:./_-]*.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SenMLFormat.valid_name"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/formats/#Choosing-a-format","page":"Choosing a format","text":"In this chapter you'll write the same pack in every SenML media type and compare their sizes.\n\npack = Pack([\n    Record(bn = \"urn:dev:mac:0024befffe804ff1:\", bt = 1_700_000_000, bu = \"Cel\",\n           n = \"room\", v = 21.5),\n    Record(n = \"setpoint\", v = 21.0),\n    Record(n = \"heating\", u = \"/\", v = 0.4),\n])\n\nfor media in instances(MediaType)\n    bytes = SenMLFormat.encode(media, pack)\n    @assert SenMLFormat.decode(media, bytes) == pack\n    @printf(\"%-28s CoAP %3d: %3d bytes\\n\", content_type(media),\n            coap_content_format(media), length(bytes))\nend\n\n# EXI is bit-packed by default. Byte-aligned EXI is a little bigger, but every\n# value starts on a byte boundary.\naligned = SenMLFormat.Exi.encode(pack; alignment = :byte_aligned)\nprintln(\"EXI byte-aligned: \", length(aligned), \" bytes\")\n\n# A content type from an HTTP header or a CoAP option picks the format.\nprintln(media_type(\"application/senml+cbor\"))\napplication/senml+json       CoAP 110: 143 bytes\napplication/sensml+json      CoAP 111: 143 bytes\napplication/senml+cbor       CoAP 112:  91 bytes\napplication/sensml+cbor      CoAP 113:  92 bytes\napplication/senml+xml        CoAP 310: 202 bytes\napplication/sensml+xml       CoAP 311: 202 bytes\napplication/senml-exi        CoAP 114:  79 bytes\napplication/sensml-exi       CoAP 115:  79 bytes\nEXI byte-aligned: 98 bytes\nSENML_CBOR\n\nSenMLFormat.encode and SenMLFormat.decode take a MediaType, so a server can pick the format from a request's Content-Type header or CoAP Content-Format option. media_type turns either into a MediaType, and returns nothing for one it doesn't know.","title":"Choosing a format"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/formats/#Which-one-to-use","page":"Choosing a format","text":"Format Good for\nJSON Web APIs and people reading logs. Every language can read it.\nCBOR Small devices and CoAP: compact, and simple to write.\nXML Systems that already speak XML.\nEXI The smallest messages, for networks where every byte counts.\n\nThe SenSML media types mark the data as a stream. The bytes are the same as SenML, except in CBOR, where SenSML uses an array with no length up front (one byte more here), so the writer doesn't need to know how many records are coming.","title":"Which one to use"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/formats/#EXI-options","page":"Choosing a format","text":"SenML EXI is strict, schema-informed EXI with the schema of RFC 8428 section 8. SenMLFormat writes the header with its options (schema ID \"a\"), as the RFC asks, so any EXI reader with that schema can read it. Keywords of SenMLFormat.Exi.encode:\n\nalignment = :bit_packed (the default) is the smallest; :byte_aligned puts every value on a byte boundary, so a simple sensor can fill in a template by hand, as the RFC's second section 8 example shows.\ncookie = true writes the $EXI cookie (the RFC advises against it over CoAP and HTTP).\nextensions = :drop (the default) leaves out extensions, which strict EXI has no place for; :error refuses a pack that has any.\n\nSenMLFormat.Exi.decode takes out_of_band for streams whose header leaves the options out.\n\n","title":"EXI options"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/validate/#Checking-packs","page":"Checking packs","text":"In this chapter you'll see what the RFC's rules refuse, and how to ask for more.\n\nAn HVAC unit reports its supply air temperature, its compressor, and its fan:\n\n# `validate` returns nothing when the pack is good and throws a SenMLError when\n# it breaks a rule. This prints which.\nfunction check(what, pack; options...)\n    try\n        validate(pack; options...)\n        println(what, \": ok\")\n    catch e\n        e isa SenMLError || rethrow()\n        println(what, \": \", e.kind, \" (record \", e.record, \")\")\n    end\nend\n\nbase = \"urn:dev:mac:0024befffe804ff3:\"\ncheck(\"good pack\", Pack([\n    Record(bn = base, n = \"supply-air\", u = \"Cel\", v = 12.8),\n    Record(n = \"compressor\", vb = true),\n]))\n\n# A record needs a value (or a sum).\ncheck(\"no value\", Pack([Record(bn = base, n = \"fan\")]))\n\n# Labels ending in \"_\" must be understood, so an unknown one is refused.\ncheck(\"unknown _ label\", Pack([Record(bn = base, n = \"fan\", v = 0.65,\n                                      extensions = [\"mode_\" => \"eco\"])]))\n\n# \"RPM\" is not in the SenML units registry. RFC 8428 allows it, unless you ask\n# for registered units only.\nrpm = Pack([Record(bn = base, n = \"fan-speed\", u = \"RPM\", v = 900)])\ncheck(\"RPM\", rpm)\ncheck(\"RPM, registered units only\", rpm; registered_units = true)\ngood pack: ok\nno value: MissingValue (record 1)\nunknown _ label: MustUnderstand (record 1)\nRPM: ok\nRPM, registered units only: UnregisteredUnit (record 1)\n\ndecode runs the same checks while it reads, and encode runs them before it writes, so you only call validate yourself for packs you build or change. Records are counted from 1 in errors.","title":"Checking packs"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/validate/#The-rules","page":"Checking packs","text":"These come from RFC 8428 section 4:\n\nA pack has at least one record.\nA record has a value or a sum, unless it holds only base fields.\nEach record has at most one value (v, vs, vb or vd), and each label at most once.\nA record needs a name or a base name, and the full name must be a valid name.\nNumbers must be finite (no NaN or infinity).\nThe version (bver) must be a positive integer, the same in every record, and not newer than 10.\nA label that ends in _ and that SenMLFormat does not know is refused.","title":"The rules"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/validate/#Units","page":"Checking packs","text":"RFC 8428 does not require registered units, so \"RPM\" passes by default. registered_units = true only allows the units of the SenML Units registry (Table 6 of section 12.1). lookup_unit tells you what a unit means.\n\nThe registry in SenMLFormat is RFC 8428's Table 6. The units added later by RFC 8798 are not in it yet.","title":"Units"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/validate/#Checking-a-stream","page":"Checking packs","text":"For records that arrive one at a time, a Validator checks each record with check!, and finish checks the end of the pack.\n\n","title":"Checking a stream"},{"category":"section","location":"api/resolve/#Resolving","page":"Resolving","text":"","title":"Resolving"},{"category":"section","location":"api/resolve/#Fragment-identifiers","page":"Resolving","text":"","title":"Fragment identifiers"},{"category":"function","location":"api/resolve/#SenMLFormat.resolve","page":"Resolving","text":"resolve(pack; now = nothing) -> Vector{ResolvedRecord}\n\nValidates the pack, resolves every record and sorts them by time, as section 4.6 asks. The sort is stable: records with equal times keep their order. now resolves relative times; without it they are an error.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SenMLFormat.resolve"},{"category":"type","location":"api/resolve/#SenMLFormat.ResolvedRecord","page":"Resolving","text":"ResolvedRecord\n\nA record with every base value applied and an absolute time. Fields: name (base name + name), unit (or the base unit in force), value (numbers with the base value added), sum (base sum + sum, when either is present), time (absolute, seconds since the Unix epoch), update_time, version (the pack's version when it is not 10, as section 4.6 asks), extensions (regular ones only).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SenMLFormat.ResolvedRecord"},{"category":"type","location":"api/resolve/#SenMLFormat.Resolver","page":"Resolving","text":"Resolver(; now = nothing)\n\nApplies base values one record at a time (resolve!). It works the same for packs and for SenSML streams. now resolves relative times: the time the pack (or, in a stream, the record) was sent, in seconds since the Unix epoch.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SenMLFormat.Resolver"},{"category":"function","location":"api/resolve/#SenMLFormat.resolve!","page":"Resolving","text":"resolve!(res::Resolver, record) -> Union{ResolvedRecord,Nothing}\n\nApplies the record's base fields, then resolves it. Returns nothing for a record with only base fields. The record should have passed validation.\n\nThe steps follow section 4.6: name = bn + n; unit = u, or else bu; value = bv + v; time = bt + t, plus \"now\" when the total is relative.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SenMLFormat.resolve!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/resolve/#SenMLFormat.set_now!","page":"Resolving","text":"set_now!(r::Resolver, now)\n\nChanges \"now\" for the records that follow. In a SenSML stream, \"now\" is set for each record (section 4.8).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SenMLFormat.set_now!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/resolve/#SenMLFormat.to_record","page":"Resolving","text":"to_record(r::ResolvedRecord) -> Record\n\nThe resolved record as a plain record (bver carries the version when it is not 10), so a resolved pack can be written in any format.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SenMLFormat.to_record"},{"category":"function","location":"api/resolve/#SenMLFormat.is_relative_time","page":"Resolving","text":"True when t is a relative time (below 2^28).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SenMLFormat.is_relative_time"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/resolve/#SenMLFormat.ABSOLUTE_TIME_THRESHOLD","page":"Resolving","text":"2^28 (268,435,456). Times at or above this are absolute (seconds since the Unix epoch). Times below it are relative to \"now\" (section 4.5.3).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SenMLFormat.ABSOLUTE_TIME_THRESHOLD"},{"category":"type","location":"api/resolve/#SenMLFormat.RecordSelector","page":"Resolving","text":"RecordSelector\n\nA parsed rec= fragment identifier (section 9.1 has examples). ranges holds (first, last) pairs, 1-based; last is typemax(Int) for n-*.\n\nMake one with parse(RecordSelector, \"rec=3-5,10,19-*\") (a leading # is allowed) or tryparse.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SenMLFormat.RecordSelector"},{"category":"function","location":"api/resolve/#SenMLFormat.selects","page":"Resolving","text":"selects(sel::RecordSelector, position, len)\n\nWhether the 1-based position is selected in a pack of len records.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SenMLFormat.selects"},{"category":"function","location":"api/resolve/#SenMLFormat.selected","page":"Resolving","text":"selected(sel::RecordSelector, len) -> Vector{Int}\n\nThe selected 1-based positions in a pack of len records, ascending.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SenMLFormat.selected"},{"category":"section","location":"appendix/rfc-choices/#Where-the-RFC-is-unclear","page":"Where the RFC is unclear","text":"SenMLFormat is a port of rsenml 0.9 (its fixtures come from rsenml 0.9.2). Its rules, its reading of the unclear points of RFC 8428 and its output bytes are rsenml's; see Where the RFC is unclear in rsenml's guide, and its CONTRIBUTING.md, for the reasons behind them. This page lists those choices in short, then what is different because this is Julia.","title":"Where the RFC is unclear"},{"category":"section","location":"appendix/rfc-choices/#Layout","page":"Where the RFC is unclear","text":"rsenml crate SenMLFormat\nrsenml-core src/core/*.jl, one file per rsenml module\nrsenml-json src/json.jl, module SenMLFormat.Json\nrsenml-cbor src/cbor.jl, module SenMLFormat.Cbor\nrsenml-xml src/xml.jl, module SenMLFormat.Xml\nrsenml-exi + generated/senml.rs src/exi.jl, module SenMLFormat.Exi + src/generated/senml_tables.jl\nrsenml (facade) src/facade.jl: SenMLFormat.decode, SenMLFormat.encode, SenMLFormat.encode_unchecked\nrsenml-tests test/\n\nThe format modules are Json, Cbor, Xml and Exi, not JSON or XML, so they do not clash with the JSON.jl and XML.jl packages in a user's code.","title":"Layout"},{"category":"section","location":"appendix/rfc-choices/#The-same-readings-as-rsenml","page":"Where the RFC is unclear","text":"Records of base fields only. A record with no regular field at all (like the first record of section 5.1.7) is context for the records after it: it needs no value or name and resolves to nothing. Every other record needs a value or a sum (section 4.2).\nVersion. A record's version is the last bver at or before it (default 10); it must be the same in every record; newer than 10 is refused, older is accepted (the RFC's own example has bver: 5).\nNames (4.5.1): base name + name must match [A-Za-z0-9][A-Za-z0-9:./_-]*.\nResolution (4.6): bv adds to numeric values only; sum is bs + s when either is present; relative times need the caller's now; unknown base extensions are dropped, regular ones kept; output sorted by time (stable).\nUnits need not be registered; Validator(registered_units = true) makes it a rule. The registry is Table 6 only.\nNumbers must be finite in every format.\nData values are strict unpadded base64url.\nCBOR keys. A text key that spells a registered label is an unknown field, ignored with its value. Strings must be definite-length. SenML packs are definite arrays; SenSML streams may be indefinite and are written so.\nXML. UTF-8 only; a DTD is refused; comments, processing instructions and whitespace are allowed between records; attributes in other namespaces are ignored; bver is xs:int.\nEXI. Options with schemaId \"a\" in the header; streams without options only  with out_of_band; extensions dropped (or refused with extensions = :error);  -0.0 is written as 0, as EXI has no negative zero.\n\nWriters put fields in the order bn bt bu bv bs bver n u t ut s v vs vb vd, then extensions, without whitespace. Numbers use the shortest text that reads back as the same double; CBOR numbers take the smallest exact form.","title":"The same readings as rsenml"},{"category":"section","location":"appendix/rfc-choices/#What-is-different-in-Julia","page":"Where the RFC is unclear","text":"Records. Record is immutable, built with keywords (Record(n = \"temp\", v = 23.1)); each field is nothing when absent. Its value is one field value::Union{Nothing,Value} with Value = Union{Float64,String,Bool,Vector{UInt8}}; the v, vs, vb and vd keywords pick the kind. Records compare by kind, so vb = true is not v = 1 (in Julia true == 1.0). Pack is an AbstractVector{Record}. Records are numbered from 1 in errors; byte offsets count from 0, like rsenml's.\nErrors. One exception type, SenMLError. Its kind is a Symbol named after rsenml's Error variant (:Syntax, :MissingValue, ...), so a test can check that both libraries give the same verdict. The decoders throw nothing else, on any input.\nJSON, CBOR and XML readers written here. rsenml uses nanojson, minicbor and xmlparser. SenMLFormat does not use JSON.jl, CBOR.jl or XML.jl: each reader is written here with the grammar rules of the Rust crate it stands for, quirks included (for example a value cut off inside a CBOR map is :Syntax, as in minicbor, not :Incomplete), so the 66 shared vectors get the same verdict. XML.jl is not used: it gives no error offsets and accepts input xmlparser refuses.\nShortest numbers. Julia's repr(Float64) is Ryu, like Rust's, but Rust rounds exact halfway cases up and Julia does not; shortest() applies Rust's rule. Checked equal to Rust on 300 000 doubles.\nEXI through EXIFormat. EXIFormat 0.1 is a hard dependency, from the General registry. The grammar tables are made by EXIFormat's schema compiler from the RFC 8428 section 8 XSD with schemaId \"a\" (scripts/gen-tables.sh) and committed. The RFC is not committed: scripts/fetch-standards.sh downloads it and takes the schema out of it. EXIFormat errors become SenMLErrors.\nNot ported: encode_into (rsenml's fixed-buffer writers, for no_std). In Julia, encode returns a new Vector{UInt8}. The JSON, CBOR and XML per-record writers (encode_record, StreamEncoder) write to any IO; EXI's StreamEncoder returns the bytes from finish!. The no_std and footprint work does not apply.\nFacade names. SenMLFormat.decode, SenMLFormat.encode and SenMLFormat.encode_unchecked are not exported (common names). They take bytes or a string.\nPrecompiling. A PrecompileTools workload reads and writes a small pack in all eight media types, so the first call in a new session is quick.\n\n","title":"What is different in Julia"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/streaming/#Streaming-with-SenSML","page":"Streaming with SenSML","text":"In this chapter you'll write records one at a time and read them back as their bytes arrive.\n\nA tank level sensor sends a reading every minute over a connection that stays open. The writer doesn't know how many records there will be, and the reader wants each one as soon as it comes:\n\n# The sensor writes one record each minute into the same stream.\nwire = IOBuffer()\nencoder = SenMLFormat.Json.StreamEncoder()\nSenMLFormat.Json.record!(encoder, wire,\n    Record(bn = \"urn:dev:mac:0024befffe804ff4:tank:\", bt = 1_700_000_000,\n           n = \"level\", u = \"m\", v = 1.82))\nfor (minute, level) in ((1, 1.81), (2, 1.79))\n    SenMLFormat.Json.record!(encoder, wire, Record(n = \"level\", t = minute * 60, v = level))\nend\nSenMLFormat.Json.finish!(encoder, wire)\nbytes = take!(wire)\nprintln(String(copy(bytes)))\n\n# The reader gets the bytes in pieces of 16, as a network might deliver them.\ndecoder = SenMLFormat.Json.StreamDecoder()\nfor piece in Iterators.partition(bytes, 16)\n    push!(decoder, collect(piece))\n    while (record = SenMLFormat.Json.next_record!(decoder)) !== nothing\n        println(\"got \", record.n, \" = \", repr(record.value))\n    end\nend\nSenMLFormat.Json.finish(decoder)\nprintln(\"stream finished: \", SenMLFormat.Json.isfinished(decoder))\n[{\"bn\":\"urn:dev:mac:0024befffe804ff4:tank:\",\"bt\":1700000000,\"n\":\"level\",\"u\":\"m\",\"v\":1.82},{\"n\":\"level\",\"t\":60,\"v\":1.81},{\"n\":\"level\",\"t\":120,\"v\":1.79}]\ngot level = 1.82\ngot level = 1.81\ngot level = 1.79\nstream finished: true\n\npush! adds bytes, and next_record! returns a record once all of it has arrived, or nothing to say \"wait for more\". Each record is checked as it comes out, so a bad record is reported where it is, not at the end of the stream.","title":"Streaming with SenSML"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/streaming/#What-each-format-offers","page":"Streaming with SenSML","text":" JSON CBOR XML EXI\nStreamEncoder (write record by record) yes yes yes yes\nRecordReader (read record by record, whole input) yes yes yes yes\nStreamDecoder (read as bytes arrive) yes yes no no\n\nThe JSON, CBOR and XML StreamEncoders write to any IO; the CBOR one writes an array with no length up front, as SenSML in CBOR should. The EXI StreamEncoder returns its bytes from finish!.\n\nRecordReader is for a whole message that you don't want to turn into a Pack first, for example to look at each record and keep only some.\n\n","title":"What each format offers"},{"category":"section","location":"api/media/#Media-types","page":"Media types","text":"","title":"Media types"},{"category":"type","location":"api/media/#SenMLFormat.MediaType","page":"Media types","text":"MediaType\n\nThe eight SenML and SenSML media types: SENML_JSON, SENSML_JSON, SENML_CBOR, SENSML_CBOR, SENML_XML, SENSML_XML, SENML_EXI, SENSML_EXI. For each format, SenML comes before SenSML.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SenMLFormat.MediaType"},{"category":"function","location":"api/media/#SenMLFormat.media_type","page":"Media types","text":"media_type(format::Symbol, streaming::Bool) -> MediaType\nmedia_type(content_type::AbstractString) -> Union{MediaType,Nothing}\nmedia_type(coap_content_format::Integer) -> Union{MediaType,Nothing}\n\nThe media type of a format (:json, :cbor, :xml, :exi), of a content type (case and parameters such as ; charset=... are ignored), or of a CoAP Content-Format ID.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SenMLFormat.media_type"},{"category":"function","location":"api/media/#SenMLFormat.content_type","page":"Media types","text":"The media type name, for example application/senml+json.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SenMLFormat.content_type"},{"category":"function","location":"api/media/#SenMLFormat.coap_content_format","page":"Media types","text":"The CoAP Content-Format ID (Table 8 in RFC 8428 section 12.5).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SenMLFormat.coap_content_format"},{"category":"function","location":"api/media/#SenMLFormat.media_format","page":"Media types","text":"The format of a media type: :json, :cbor, :xml or :exi.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SenMLFormat.media_format"},{"category":"function","location":"api/media/#SenMLFormat.is_streaming","page":"Media types","text":"SenSML (streaming, RFC 8428 section 4.8) rather than SenML.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SenMLFormat.is_streaming"}]
}
